sequence07	MetaGeneAnnotator	CDS	182049	182504	.	+	0	ID=MGA_3924;product=phosphohistidine phosphatase SixA;inference=COORDINATES:ab initio prediction:MetaGeneAnnotator,similar to AA sequence:RefSeq:WP_005769602.1;translation=MKVWVLRHGEAQSRARSDAERELTAHGREEVQKSAMHLTGKPVQRILASPYVRAQQTAELVRQSLGFDAPVVTVPWLTPDGSPRQVLAQLETLGVDEVLLVSHQPLVGELIGVLAHGSAQQAEPMSTASLAELEGEFVAAGTMTLNSVRHV;locus_tag=LOCUS_39160;gene=sixA;note=WP_005769602.1 phosphohistidine phosphatase SixA (Pseudomonas syringae group) [pid:94.7%25%2C q_cov:100.0%25%2C s_cov:100.0%25%2C Eval:3.1e-76],Domain:cl11399:HP Histidine phosphatase domain found in a functionally diverse set of proteins%2C mostly phosphatases%3B contains a His residue which is phosphorylated during the reaction. The histidine phosphatase superfamily is so named because catalysis centers on a conserved His residue that is transiently phosphorylated during the catalytic cycle. Other conserved residues contribute to a 'phosphate pocket' and interact with the phospho group of substrate before%2C during and after its transfer to the His residue. Structure and sequence analyses show that different families contribute different additional residues to the 'phosphate pocket' and%2C more surprisingly%2C differ in the position%2C in sequence and in three dimensions%2C of a catalytically essential acidic residue. The superfamily may be divided into two main branches.The smaller branch 2 contains predominantly eukaryotic proteins. The catalytic functions in members include phytase%2C glucose-1-phosphatase and multiple inositol polyphosphate phosphatase. The in vivo roles of the mammalian acid phosphatases in branch 2 are not fully understood%2C although activity against lysophosphatidic acid and tyrosine-phosphorylated proteins has been demonstrated. [Superfamily%2C Aligned:1-147%2C Eval:6.7e-35%2C score:116.6],MGA_3924
