sequence08	MetaGeneAnnotator	CDS	2385	3485	.	+	0	ID=MGA_3942;product=amidinotransferase;inference=COORDINATES:ab initio prediction:MetaGeneAnnotator,similar to AA sequence:RefSeq:WP_005614684.1;;locus_tag=LOCUS_39330;note=WP_005614684.1 amidinotransferase (Pseudomonas) [pid:88.8%25%2C q_cov:100.0%25%2C s_cov:100.0%25%2C Eval:9.2e-201],Domain:cl19186:Amidinotransf Amidinotransferase. Peptidyl-arginine deiminase (PAD) enzymes catalyze the deimination of the guanidino group from carboxy-terminal arginine residues of various peptides to produce ammonia. PAD from Porphyromonas gingivalis (PPAD) appears to be evolutionarily unrelated to mammalian PAD (pfam03068)%2C which is a metalloenzyme. PPAD is thought to belong to the same superfamily as aminotransferase and arginine deiminase%2C and to form an alpha/beta propeller structure. This family has previously been named PPADH (Porphyromonas peptidyl-arginine deiminase homologs). The predicted catalytic residues in PPAD are Asp130%2C Asp187%2C His236%2C Asp238 and Cys351. These are absolutely conserved with the exception of Asp187 which is absent in two family members. PPAD is also able to catalyze the deimination of free L-arginine%2C but has primarily peptidyl-arginine specificity. It may have a FMN cofactor. [Superfamily%2C Aligned:61-354%2C Eval:3.4e-09%2C score:54.3],MGA_3942
sequence08	MetaGeneAnnotator	CDS	3627	4877	.	+	0	ID=MGA_3943;product=HPr kinase;inference=COORDINATES:ab initio prediction:MetaGeneAnnotator,similar to AA sequence:RefSeq:WP_005769301.1;;locus_tag=LOCUS_39340;note=WP_005769301.1 HPr kinase (Pseudomonas syringae group) [pid:69.5%25%2C q_cov:99.3%25%2C s_cov:99.0%25%2C Eval:3.0e-166],Domain:cl27719:Biotin_carb_N Biotin carboxylase%2C N-terminal domain. Carbamoyl-phosphate synthase (CPSase) catalyzes the first committed step in pyrimidine%2C arginine%2C and urea biosynthesis. In general%2C it is a glutamine-dependent enzyme%2C EC 6.3.5.5%2C termed CPSase II in eukaryotes. An exception is the mammalian mitochondrial urea-cycle form%2C CPSase I%2C in which the glutamine amidotransferase domain active site Cys on the small subunit has been lost%2C and the enzyme is ammonia-dependent. In both CPSase I and the closely related%2C glutamine-dependent CPSase III (allosterically activated by acetyl-glutamate) demonstrated in some other vertebrates%2C the small and large chain regions are fused in a single polypeptide chain. This model represents the large chain of glutamine-hydrolysing carbamoyl-phosphate synthases%2C or the corresponding regions of larger%2C multifunctional proteins%2C as found in all domains of life%2C and CPSase I forms are considered exceptions within the family. In several thermophilic species (Methanobacterium thermoautotrophicum%2C Methanococcus jannaschii%2C Aquifex aeolicus)%2C the large subunit appears split%2C at different points%2C into two separate genes. [Purines%2C pyrimidines%2C nucleosides%2C and nucleotides%2C Pyrimidine ribonucleotide biosynthesis] [Superfamily%2C Aligned:102-316%2C Eval:1.4e-10%2C score:59.6%2C C-term missing],MGA_3943
